## Actions, in order

**1. Unblock the executor** (Bash, foreground; returns in seconds)

```bash
herdr agent prompt wgka "$(cat <<'EOF'
Step 6 answer: Option 1 — allow only the WireGuard /25, then `deny all`.
This is the approved design, not a new choice: CLAUDE.md, "The WireGuard keepalive relay" -> "only `allow <WG /25>; deny all;`".
Open docs/superpowers/specs/2026-09-27-ccs-wg-keepalive-relay-design.md and quote the matching line in your report. If the spec says otherwise, stop and report.
Derive the /25 from ccs_vpn_client_subnet (upper half). No literal 10.77.1.128/25 in the role: ccs-ops must render 10.77.2.128/25 from the same template.
Keep the allow-list in one place. Advisor ballot B8 is ratifying this in parallel; a different result is a one-line change before review.
Continue with superpowers:test-driven-development (failing render test first), then superpowers:verification-before-completion. Carry on through the remaining plan steps. Reply in English.
EOF
)" --wait --until working --timeout 60000
```

**2. Ballot B8 to the idle advisor** (Bash, foreground; seconds)

```bash
herdr agent prompt adv-fable "$(cat <<'EOF'
Ballot B8. Read-only, vote independently, modify nothing.
Branch feat/wg-keepalive-relay, plan step 6: source allow-list of the WireGuard keepalive relay (conf.d/wg-keepalive.conf).
Option 1: allow only the WireGuard /25 (upper half of ccs_vpn_client_subnet), deny all.
Option 2: allow the whole VPN /24, same as the front-door allow-list.
Check against the approved spec and CLAUDE.md.
Reply in English: VOTE: 1|2 / REASON: max 3 lines / RISK: what the executor must watch.
EOF
)" --wait --until working --timeout 60000
```

**3. Three event-driven waits** (Bash, each `run_in_background: true`)

```bash
herdr agent wait adv-fable --timeout 1200000
herdr agent wait adv-codex --timeout 1200000
herdr agent wait wgka --timeout 3600000
```

When the `adv-codex` wait fires, I read B7, then send it the same B8 text. I do not interrupt B7.

**4. Acceptance sheet** (Agent tool, `model: sonnet`)

> Write the operator's acceptance sheet for the WG keepalive relay canary on ccs-it. Copy the pass criteria exactly from the acceptance gate in docs/superpowers/plans/2026-09-28-ccs-wg-keepalive-relay.md; invent none. Steps: connect with your own WireGuard profile, run `ping -i 0.2 172.27.96.2` for 3 minutes, what pass/fail looks like. One page, plain words. Write nothing to the repo. Reply in English.

**5. Backlog**: edit `orchestrator/backlog.md` (step 6 unblocked, B8 open, acceptance pending on the operator), run `orchestrator/build-backlog.sh` (foreground), update the Artifact.

**6. Status note to the operator** (chat text, not a question)

> Status 01:42 — nothing needed from you now. The relay's allow-list question is answered by the approved design (WireGuard clients only); advisors are ratifying it in the background. One step is yours alone: acceptance, which is your WireGuard profile plus `ping -i 0.2 172.27.96.2` for 3 minutes. I will not wake you for it. Once the canary (the relay switched on for ccs-it only) is live and verified from the hypervisor, one poll with exact steps will be waiting. Until then it is reported as "deployed, not accepted", and nothing goes to ccs-ops.

**7. Last action of the night**, once nothing else is owed: publish the sheet as an Artifact, then AskUserQuestion: "Canary is live on ccs-it, server-side checks green. Your ping result?" Options: **Passed** / **Failed — saw drops or a reconnect** / **Not yet — keep it unaccepted**.

## Why

- **Not my decision:** the operator-approved design already fixes the /25, so answering the executor is relaying it. My own agreement and last night's precedent are not the basis.
- **The two-advisor rule still holds:** B8 runs with neutral wording. Step 6 is a one-line, unmerged, undeployed change, so ratification can land after the work. Anything short of 2/2 for Option 1 stops before review, and a split joins the final poll.
- **No idle time, no wake-up:** waiting for `adv-codex` would burn about 25 minutes of the executor's time box, and the question is trivia by the operator's own definition.
- **Background waits:** the harness wakes me on each event, so my session stays free.
- **Acceptance is the operator's:** hard rule #17 bars a session from the VPN path, and server-side checks are evidence, not acceptance. I neither fake it nor wake them.
- **The poll goes last:** it blocks my session, so issuing it now would stall the canary the operator asked for tonight.
